(**Q1: Explain PARASITE.**) Tj
ET
0.15 0.39 0.92 RG
2. w
45. 515.8899999999999864 m
45. 451.8899999999999864 l
S
1. w
BT
/F3 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.278 0.333 0.412 rg
55. 505.8899999999999864 Td
(Answer: PARASITE is a compiler-inspired static analysis system built to detect structural code plagiarism. Unlike) Tj
T* (simple text-differencing tools, PARASITE analyzes code at multiple levels of program representation: Lexical tokens,) Tj
T* (Abstract Syntax Trees \(AST\), Control Flow Graphs \(CFG\), and Data Flow dependency chains. It computes a) Tj
T* (balanced, weighted similarity score \($35\\%\\text{ AST} + 25\\%\\text{ Token} + 20\\%\\text{ CFG} + 10\\%\\text{ Data Flow}) Tj
T* (+ 10\\%\\text{ LCS}$\) that remains resilient against variable renaming, formatting changes, and statement reordering.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 10 Tf
11.5 TL
0.059 0.09 0.165 rg
40. 431.8899999999999864 Td
(**Q2: Why did you choose plagiarism detection?**) Tj
ET
0.15 0.39 0.92 RG
2. w
45. 417.8899999999999864 m
45. 377.8899999999999864 l
S
1. w
BT
/F3 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.278 0.333 0.412 rg
55. 407.8899999999999864 Td
(Answer: Standard plagiarists bypass traditional text diffing tools in minutes simply by renaming variables or) Tj
T* (reformatting code. I wanted to apply core compiler design principles \(Lexing, Parsing, Intermediate Representations,) Tj
T* (Symbol Tables, Static Analysis\) to solve a practical, real-world software engineering problem.) Tj
